    "body": "### What needs to be documented or fixed?\n\nADR-0006 was revised in #64. A tenant gets one catalog by default, and a project can get its own catalog.\nADR-0004, ADR-0005 and ADR-0007 still assume one catalog per project but the discussion on this issue showed a larger gap.\nThe ADRs describe access for each project or schema, but nothing links a user or tenant to data access yet.\n\nToday every app reads with its own credentials, and every user gets the same access.\nZitadel only decides who can reach a service.\nNothing creates project roles in Zitadel, so every user who can log in reaches every service.\n\nThe ADRs also contradict each other on this point.\n\n- ADR-0006 says a shared tenant catalog separates projects \"application-enforced\", but names no application. A direct read (Tier A) passes no enforcement point.\n- ADR-0008 and ADR-0009 (option 1) assume a Postgres reader role for each project. That does not fit a shared tenant catalog.\n- ADR-0009 says materialization never crosses projects. That is only true if a user gets a shared catalog when all its projects are granted.\n- ADR-0007 says the enforcement point sets the memory limit of a Tier A read. The client sets that limit in its own process, the flaw that ADR-0008 names for `READ_ONLY`.\n\n### Page or section\n\n- `docs/adr/0004-data-organization-and-ingestion.md` (`catalog = project`, `schema = layer`)\n- `docs/adr/0005-authorization-and-data-access.md` (access by attaching catalogs per project)\n- `docs/adr/0006-deployment-and-project-isolation-model.md` (\"application-enforced\")\n- `docs/adr/0007-compute-and-scaling.md` (catalogs per project, the Tier A memory limit)\n- `docs/adr/0008-identity-propagation-and-data-contracts.md` (reader role per project)\n- `docs/adr/0009-data-plane-credential-materialization.md` (option 1, \"never crosses projects\")\n\n### Suggested improvement\n\n#171 gives the first version of access, which is per service. This spike is the next step, from service to data.\n\nA spike by the core maintainers, in this order.\n\n1. Connect identity to data access. Decide how a Zitadel user and its tenant link to the data, and which claims carry them.\n2. Define a simple access tree on top of that. A user or role gets read, write or both on a project, or on specific tables in it.\n\n   ```text\n   tenant\n   └── project            read | write\n       └── table          read | write\n   ```\n\n3. Evaluate the access path that enforces the tree.\n   - The API checks every read and write.\n   - Postgres roles limit access to the DuckLake catalog. One Postgres instance also holds Zitadel, Dagster and Marquez, so a direct connection exposes more than the catalog.\n   - A Quack server (ADR-0009, option 2) holds the connection, so no app or user connects to Postgres directly.\n   - A combination is possible, such as the API for writes and Quack for reads.\n   - Decide whether SRDP builds the access decision itself or uses an existing authorization tool.\n4. Decide how a project declares this tree, through `srdp-project.toml` and `srdp-setup` or through the API.\n5. Decide what the operator manages and what SRDP manages.\n6. Update ADR-0004 to ADR-0009 to this model. Mark what is built and what is target design, as page 11 does.\n7. Open issues for the follow-up work.\n\nCompute access is out of scope and comes later.\n\n#132 stays separate. It gives each platform service its own Postgres role, which every model needs.\n",
